'''Trinacria''' ({{Langx|mxi|تْرِينَكْرِيَا|Trinákria}}; from [[W:Greek language|Aetolian]] ''Τρινακρία''; {{Literally|three headlands}}; {{Lang-kp|Tamí Wakarí}}; {{Literal translation|three tongues}}) is a [[Boroughs of Castejón|borough]] of [[Castejón]], within the [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]]. It is the largest borough geographically and is bounded by the west by [[Salto Bay]], and shares a land border with the [[Araucaria]]n region [[Luzamontaña]]. The borough is the second-least populous in Castejón (ahead of [[Hisbanya]]), with an estimated one million residents in 2026. Trinacria is notable as a center of innovation, its {{W|urban design}}, [[W:Mediterranean climate|Mediterranean climate]], and low cost of living for migrants compared to the rest of the [[Castejón Metropolis|Metropolis]]. Since its annexation, Trinacria has attracted significant investment from the growing Baliscan high tech industry, and has become popular with students and immigrants due to it's high [[W:Quality of life|quality of life]] and the availability of [[Housing in Balisca|social housing]]. 

During the initial discussions around the annexation of Trinacria, [[W:NIMBYism|NIMBYism]] arose from a sizable number of groups in Trinacria, including several members of the [[W:Community Development Council|communal development council]]. The area at the time was primarily populated by individuals unable to afford increasing [[Baliscan Social Living Index|cost of living]] in the more dense boroughs across Salto Bay, who asserted that the annexation would invite [[W:Gentrification|gentrification]] and [[W:Environmental degradation|environmental degradation]]. Even following the success of the annexation referendum, NIMBY groups aimed to derail development with disruptive methods (such as [[W:Frivolous litigation|frivolous litigation]]) which led to significant delays for new housing construction, transit expansion and cycling infrastructure. NIMBYs often cited restrictions on buildings' shadows, concerns over car traffic, parking and crime as reasons for opposing projects. A coalition of migrant advocacy organizations, residents and communal delegates asserted that new housing construction would make housing more affordable, thereby reducing displacement, personal property tax burdens. Ultimately, grassroots [[W:YIMBY|YIMBY]] organizations would successfully push for continued development, with substantial expansion of affordable housing, transit options, and the end of car-centric planning. 

During the construction boom of the early 1980s, the borough was the epicenter of [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake]]. Following the dissolution of the [[Ampurias Housing Authority|Trinacria Housing Authority]] in 1985, zoning laws were abolished and development of Trinacria was relinquished to barrio and commune level cooperatives such as the [[Sarria Collective]]. Politically, the borough has strongly supported [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] candidates, and all of its [[commune district]]s are represented by delegates associated with the [[Confederal Left]].
