==Economy==
{{Main|Economy of Castejón}}
[[File:Trinacria housing estate.png|left|thumb|300x300px|Trinacria's [[Berreyarza]] barrio has earned the nickname "Silicon Alley" as a result of it's dense concentration of tech startups and companies]]
The single top employer in the city is the borough administration itself, employing 5.6% (35,000+ people) of the borough's workforce, followed by [[Capitoline University]] with over 25,000 employees. The largest cooperative employer is the [[Sarria Corporation]], with 8,500 employees, as of 2018. Small co-ops with fewer than 50 employees and self-employed firms make up 85% of borough establishments, and the number of Trinacrians employed by firms of more than 1,000 employees has fallen by upwards of 50% since 2000. The growth of national and multinational {{W|big box store|big box}} and {{W|formula retail}} chains into the borough has been made intentionally difficult by a combination of civic opposition and political consensus. In an effort to buoy small emerging co-ops in Trinacria and preserve the unique retail personality of the city, the Small Business Commission started a publicity campaign in 2004 to keep a larger share of retail dollars in the local economy via the [[Autonomous commune|autonomous communes]] to limit the neighborhoods where formula retail establishments can set up shop, an effort affirmed by cantonal voters. As a result, by 2016, Trinacria was rated amongst the lowest in the country by foreign businesses in a Business Friendliness Survey.

High technology startup companies and employment are growing in Trinacria and the wider Castejón region. The technology sector has been claiming a greater share of Trinacria's economy since 2000. [[Tech:Basilio|Tech:Trinacria]], founded in 2016, is a non-profit organization which represents the borough's technology sector with administration, civic institutions, in business, and in the media, and whose primary goals are to further augment Castejón's substantial tech talent and to advocate for policies that will nurture tech companies to grow in the city. The biotechnology sector is also growing in Trinacria, based upon the city's strength in academic scientific research and public and commercial financial support.

Trinacria has a diversified service economy, with employment spread across a wide range of professional services, including [[W:Biotechnology|biotechnology]], tourism, and (increasingly) high technology. In 2016, approximately 30% of workers were employed in professional business services; 18% in leisure and hospitality; 16% in administrative services; 12% in education and health care; 11% in trade, transportation, and utilities; and 10% in financial activities. In 2019, GDP in the borough's area grew 3.8% in real terms to $### billion. Additionally, in 2019 the 18-commune [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]] had a GDP of $1.902 trillion, ranking 1st amongst all [[megaregions of Balisca]]. As of 2022, the borough was the 5th highest-income sub-regional territory in Balisca (among 6,284), with a per capita personal income of $79,172.
