==Government and politics==
[[File:USA-San Jose-City Hall-Rotunda-3.jpg|thumb|200px|[[Basilio Borough Hall|Trinacria Borough Hall]], the seat of the [[Assembly of Basilio|Assembly of Trinacria]]]]The delegates of the [[Assembly of Basilio|Assembly of Trinacria]] are elected to represent their [[Commune district|commune districts]] within the borough. Upon the death or resignation of the [[Cantonal secretary (Balisca)|borough secretary]] (who is considered "first amongst equals"), their position is filled by way of [[W:Consensus democracy|consensus]]. In 1982, [[Miryam Cervelló]] assumed the office following the death of [[Cristòfol Aupí]] during the [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake|Tava Morotí earthquake]], and was later selected by the Assembly to finish the term. In 2021, [[Domènec Amargant]] was selected by the Assembly to finish the term of [[Jaïr Casallac]], who was {{w|recall election|recalled}} by his commune district following widespread dissatisfaction with his stances during [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]]. He also later resigned, and was replaced by Assembly-member [[Sayen Calfuray]], who was later elected to a full term.

Trinacria was granted a perpetual leasehold over the [[Merced Reservoir]] in accordance with the the stipulations of the [[Good Friday Agreement]]. However, protracted disputes between the Assembly of Trinacria and the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón]] have fueled calls for the {{W|retrocession}} of Trinacria, or a full {{W|urban secession}} to form its own separate region.

Until decommissioning in the early 1970s, the borough had naval military installations at the [[Presidio, Trinacria|Presidio]], [[Guadiaro]], and the [[Embarcadero]]—a legacy still reflected in the annual celebration of [[Fleet Week|Armada Week]]. Foreign governments maintain more than thirty embassies in Trinacria, with a sizable portion of those having moved from the [[Ampurias]] borough.

The cantonal budget for fiscal year 2021–22 was $# billion, and is one of the largest sub-regional budgets in the Baliscan Confederation. The Borough of Trinacria spends more per resident than any other sub-regional area in Balisca, over $10,000 in FY 2021–2022. Cantonal administration employs around 35,000 residents.
===Local politics===
====Issues====
{{See also|Baliscan housing crisis}}Housing is a frequent topic in Trinacria's (and Castejón's) politics.

Castejón has the highest housing prices in the Baliscan Confederation. As of 2022, its median house price in the metropolis was $400,000, almost twice the average from five years earlier. Many factors contribute to the housing situation in Castejón. One of the main reasons for this is the lack of available homes to live in. The metropolitan city, from 1973 to 2023, only created 1 home for every 3 jobs created. In response, Trinacria's voters in 2021 approved both (by overwhelming margins) [[Proposition 11]] (which established a minimum of 45% of all new residential development being reserved as {{W|affordable housing}}), [[Proposition 12]] (which expropriated residential properties purchased by foreign multinational entities), and approved the borough's adoption of the [[Casajuan Plan]].

Trinacria, having some of the most "pro-YIMBY" housing laws in Castejón, has seen a rapid influx of residents, mostly students and migrants from outside of the Confederation. Additionally, it has the strongest rent controls in the entirety of Balisca. In 2022, the average price for a home in Trinacria was approximately $280,000, an increase of 0.6% over the prior month.

It has been estimated by Trinacria's council that in order for prices in Trinacria to stabilize, borough city would need around 25,000 units to reduce prices. This issue has complicated by the confederal efforts to limit or discourage {{W|suburbanization}}, {{W|urban sprawl}} and {{w|Brusselization}}, but there have been increasing expansion of more sustainable approaches such as {{w|coliving}} and {{w|cohousing}}.
